Denying a Request for Reasonable Accommodation. Under certain circumstances, a housing provider may deny a tenant's request to have
an assistance animal, even if all of the foregoing criteria have been satisfied: 1) if the assistance animal has a history of dangerous behavior;
2) if the presence of the assistance animal would result in substantial physical damage to the property of others unless the threat can be
eliminated or significantly reduced by a reasonable accommodation; (3) if allowing the assistance animal poses an undue financial and
administrative burden on the housing provider; or (4) if allowing the assistance animal fundamentally alters the nature of the provider's
operations.
Breed size and weight limits may not be applied to assistance animals. A determination that an assistance animal poses a direct threat of
harm to others or would cause substantial physical damage to the property of others must be based on the individualized assessment that
relies on objective evidence about the specific animal's actual conduct, not on mere speculation or fear about the types of harm or damage an
animal may cause or on prior experiences with other animals.
Damage Caused by Assistance Animal. While the housing provider is prohibited from collecting an additional damage deposit based on the
assistance animal living in or on the property, the tenant remains responsible for damage to the property caused by the assistance animal. If
the housing provider learns of the damage while the tenant is still residing in the property, the housing provider may treat such occurrence in
the same manner as s/he would treat the occurrence of damage to the property caused by the tenant. For example, damage to the property
may result in the housing provider serving the tenant with a three or seven-day notice of non-compliance with the rental agreement. If the
housing provider learns of the damage after the tenant has vacated the property, the housing provider may apply the damage deposit to the
cost of remediating such damage (as long as the housing provider complies with all accounting of the damage deposit as required by law)
and if such damage deposit is insufficient, may pursue all legal remedies available by law and equity against the tenant for recovery of any
remaining amount due to repair the damage.
For More Information. For more information on the the ADA, the FHAct and Section504,visit,http://portal.hud.gov/ hudportal/HUD; type in
""assistance animals"" in the search box. For advice regarding a specific situation involving an assistance animal, housing providers should
consult with their own independent legal counsel.
