TENANT ACKNOWLEDGES THAT:
1. They have received and read the pamphlet ""Bed Bug Prevention, Detection and Control,” available online at https://www.epa.gov/
sites/production/files/2014-02/documents/bed-bug-prevention-detection-control.pdf, which outlines how to detect bed bugs, how they spread,
how to prevent bed bugs and pest management.
2. The Landlord has inspected the Property and is not aware of any bed bug infestation.
3. He/she may have his/her own inspection of the Property conducted by a pest management professional before moving into the
Property and that such inspection would be at Tenant's sole cost and expense.
4. All furnishings and personal properties that will be moved into the Property will be free of bed bugs.
TENANT HEREBY AGREES TO THE FOLLOWING:
1. To check for hitch-hiking bed bugs. If Tenant(s) stays in a hotel or another home, Tenant(s) agrees to inspect clothing, luggage, shoes and
personal belongings for signs of bed bugs before re-entering the Property. After using public transportation or visiting theater, Tenant(s)
agrees to check backpacks, shoes and clothing before re-entering Property. After guests visit, Tenant(s) agree to inspect beds, bedding and
upholstered furniture for signs of bed bug infestation;
2. To report any problems immediately to Landlord. Even a few bed bugs can rapidly multiply to create a major infestation that may spread
to other properties;
3. To cooperate with Landlord and/or pest management professionals in their pet control efforts. If the Property or, in the event of multi-
family units, a neighbor's property is infested a pest management professional may be called in to eradicate the problem. The Property
must be properly prepared for treatment. Tenant must comply with recommendations and requests from the pest management
professional prior to treatment, including but not limited to:
+ Placing all bedding, drapes, curtains and small rugs in bags for transport to laundry or dry cleaners;
* Disposing of heavily infested mattresses by sealing them in plastic and disposing of them properly;
+ Emptying dressers, night stands and closets;
* Removing all items from floors. All clothing, shoes, boxes, toys, etc. should be bagged and sealed. Washable and non-washable items
should be bagged separately and used bags must be disposed of properly.
* Vacuuming all floors (including closet floors), furniture (including inside drawers and nightstands) and mattresses and box springs;
carefully remove vacuum bags, seal them tightly in plastic and discard of them properly;
* Washing all machine-washable bedding, drapes and clothing, etc. on the hottest water temperature and drying on the hightest heat setting.
Take other items to the dry cleaners; inform the dry cleaners that the items are infested;
+ Discarding any items that cannot be decontaminated;
* Clearing off furniture and moving it to the center of the room, so that technicians can easily treat furniture surfaces, walls and carpet edges,
where bed bugs congregate; leave easy access to closets.

